      "writtenOn": "2023-02-17T22:56:49Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I don\u0027t know how much you care about the extra storage occupied by both `fParent` and `fOwnedParent` in the subclass type which ultimately store the same pointer. If that\u0027s a concern, you could have `FieldLValue` declare no members, call `p.release()` instead of `p.get()` while invoking the parent destructor, and explicitly delete `fParent` (which would need to be declared as protected) in `FieldLValue`\u0027s destructor.\n\nAn alternate pattern would be to have the parent type store a `std::unique_ptr\u003cLValue\u003e`, have the subclass be the \"unowned\" type which releases `fParent` in its destructor.\n\nBoth approaches would save on the extra pointer storage but I don\u0027t know if that\u0027s worth the additional bookkeeping in destructors. Just food for thought.",
